cartridge "OBSTACLE 128/Stephen Cheng/1986"

' OBSTACLE 128
' A ColecoVision game based on "Obstacle 128", COMPUTE!'s Gazette
' November 1986 (Commodore 128 BASIC 7.0, disk 1986-11; no author is named
' in the listing, orig/obstacle 128.bas).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a pixel-for-pixel port: the rules are the listing's, rebuilt for the
' ColecoVision on an 8 x 8 cell field: raised obstacle blocks, arrow heads
' that glide from cell to cell, sound.
'
' The game (from the listing): two players each steer a line that never
' stops (one dot a pass on the C-128). The border is drawn in the players'
' colours: the top and right side in purple (player 2), the bottom and
' left side in blue (player 1), and each side stops short of a corner by
' a random 30-69 dots (line 30), leaving a gap at the top of the left side
' and at the bottom of the right side. Each round both lines start on the
' middle row (blue at x 30-69, purple at x 80-119 of 160, line 40) moving
' the same random direction. A stick held up, down, left or right turns
' (diagonals and a centred stick keep the direction; straight back is
' allowed and runs into your own line). Every pass blue moves first, then
' purple; moving onto any line or border dot crashes (lines 100, 170).
' When purple crashes blue takes one more step and may crash too (lines
' 210-230). A crash gives the OTHER player a point; both crashing gives
' both a point ("YOU BOTH CRASHED"). CRASHED is shown over the crasher's
' side, the scores at the bottom, and a new round starts by itself. The
' first player with 5 or more points and more than the other wins
' ("YOU WON", lines 280-290).
'
' Liberties: the field is 26 x 18 cells inside the border (the C-128's
' was about 157 x 197 dots), so the start columns are scaled (blue 8-13,
' purple 16-21 of 32) and the gaps are 3-6 cells; running out through a
' gap is a crash here (on the C-128 it stopped the program with an
' ILLEGAL QUANTITY error); a step is 10, 8 or 6 frames (SPEED 1-3 on the
' title; BASIC 7.0 ran at one speed); a direction pressed between two
' steps is remembered for the next step; a one-player game against a
' computer purple, which sees only up to 4 cells in a line: it keeps
' straight while it has room, turns at random one step in eight, when blocked
' turns to a random side that has room (the shorter side only when the
' other is shut), one time in two a step late and one time in four not
' at all (tuned so a human-paced player wins most games at speed 2); a crash
' burst and a pause before the next round instead of the C-128's one-
' second SLEEP; a winner's call of my own instead of the listing's PLAY
' tune; heads glide; PURPLE/CPU and BLUE named in the score line.
' Standing still is impossible (the lines never stop), and holding one
' direction runs into the border within 26 steps.
'
' Controls: controller 1 steers blue, controller 2 purple (in a one-player
' game the computer steers purple). On the title: up/down chooses a line
' (PLAYERS, SPEED, START, HOW TO PLAY, CREDITS; the cursor starts on
' START), left/right picks 1 or 2 players or the speed on their lines, a
' button starts (from PLAYERS, SPEED or START) or opens the page.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once.

